---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import TeamSimulator from '../../../components/calculator/TeamSimulator';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Team Simulator"
  description="Compare teams and skill rotations against the target dummy or a world boss using the battle simulator. Alpha — verify anything that matters in game."
>
  <main class="max-w-[100rem] mx-auto px-4 py-6">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
      <h1 class="text-xl font-semibold text-primary">Team Simulator</h1>
      <span
        class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full border border-highlight/50 bg-highlight/10 text-highlight text-[10px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider"
        title="Early build — expect bugs"
      >
        Alpha
      </span>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-secondary mb-3">
      Runs the same battle simulator used to generate the tier lists, in your browser.
    </p>

    <div class="mb-5 p-3 rounded-lg border border-highlight/40 bg-highlight/5">
      <p class="text-sm text-primary leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="font-medium">The simulator almost certainly still has bugs.</strong>
        Treat the game itself as the source of truth — if a number here matters to you,
        verify it in game.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed mt-1.5">
        Cast groups also cannot reproduce an optimal rotation — they fire on a fixed
        rule, where a solver would exploit timings no player could hit anyway. What they
        do show is which skill combinations and orderings are more or less likely to
        work.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed mt-1.5">
        So what it is good for is <em>comparison</em>: which rotation, which bonds, which
        card in the last slot. Use it to narrow down what is worth your time testing for
        real, not to predict the damage you will actually hit.
      </p>
    </div>

    <TeamSimulator client:only="react" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
